<BaseLayout title="About — OpsPrimer" description="Who writes OpsPrimer and why.">
  <h1>About</h1>

  {/* Nome real e experiencia sao OBRIGATORIOS aqui: requisito de E-E-A-T e de
      aprovacao nas redes de afiliado. NUNCA endereco residencial, telefone
      pessoal ou CPF. Ver arquivo 11 do dossie. */}

  <p>
    I'm <strong>Michel Modesto</strong>, a fullstack developer. For the past ten
    years I've built APIs, integrations and automation for small companies —
    and, often enough, the whole system around them.
  </p>

  <p>
    That's an odd background for a site about marketing automation, and it's
    exactly why I started one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Most writing about these tools comes from people who <em>evaluate</em> them.
    I'm usually the person who has to make them actually work: wire the CRM into
    the billing system, find out why a webhook fired twice, explain to a client
    why the "no-code" integration quietly dropped forty leads last Tuesday.
  </p>

  <p>
    Marketing automation gets sold as a marketing decision. Six months in, it's
    an operations problem — and the things that break are boring, specific, and
    almost never mentioned on the pricing page. Field mapping that silently
    truncates. Rate limits nobody documented. A "sync" that's really a
    fifteen-minute poll. An export that loses the one custom property the whole
    workflow depended on.
  </p>

  <p>
    So that's the angle here. Not which platform has the nicest feature grid,
    but what these tools are like to live with once real data is moving through
    them, and what it costs to leave when you outgrow one.
  </p>

  <h2>How I write</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>I only cover tools I've actually used on real work.</li>
    <li>Every tool gets a stated weakness — including the ones that pay me.</li>
    <li>Where I haven't tested something myself, I say so instead of implying I have.</li>
    <li>
      No screenshots of client data, ever. Anything you see from a real account
      is a scratch workspace with fake records.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>
    Search results for this topic are mostly written by the companies selling
    the software. That isn't a conspiracy — it's just that a vendor can never
    publish the sentence "don't buy ours, buy theirs." I can, and I do.
  </p>

